# Analyst Notes

Behavior-based detections such as frequency analysis significantly reduce false positives compared to single-event detections. While individual PowerShell executions are common in Windows environments, multiple executions over a short period may indicate attacker reconnaissance, automation, or post-exploitation activity.

This analytic demonstrates how simple statistical aggregation within Splunk can identify suspicious behavior without relying on known malware signatures or specific command-line arguments. Similar techniques are widely used in modern Security Operations Centers to detect anomalous user activity.
